{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ef5lbsnz5non37ub72yel3ly5j3tvdasw4yrif4ynurghzzjtpsa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\nA single-function library: `double next_double(cn_rnd_t *rnd)` — an xorshift128+ step whose 64-bit output is punned into the mantissa of a `1.0`-exponent double and shifted down to `[0, 1)`.\n\n## Phase A — surface mapped\n\n| Artifact | Content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | C `.so` exports exactly one symbol, `next_double`; Rust `.so` exports it too. Diff **empty**. `cn_rnd_next` is `static` in C, so correctly *not* exported. All Rust undefined symbols are libc/libgcc. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | Mechanical grep proved the C has **zero** error returns/asserts/range checks/enums/length params — it's straight-line code. 4 real rejection rows (NULL, misaligned, zero state, saturated state) + an explicit N/A justification for each generic boundary that doesn't apply. |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 3 axes derived from the source (1 entry point, 0 runtime flags, 9 input-shape sensitivities) → **18 rows**. |\n\n**Feature combinations:** `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]` table and the C has no `#ifdef`s → the powerset is the single empty combination. `verify.sh` derives it mechanically so it stays correct if features are added.\n\n## Two real divergences found and fixed (Rust only)\n\nBoth were caught by Phase C tests, not by reading code:\n\n1. **Misaligned pointer** — C does an unaligned `mov`; Rust's `&mut *rnd` tripped the compiler's *misaligned pointer dereference* check → `SIGABRT` instead of returning a value.\n2. **NULL pointer** — C faults with `SIGSEGV` (11); Rust aborted with `SIGABRT` (6), because `ptr::read_unaligned` carries a non-null precondition check.\n\nFix: `load_u64`/`store_u64` emit a bare `mov` via `core::arch::asm!` on x86-64, so the access itself faults exactly as C's does — in **debug and release** alike (rustc 1.94 enables these UB checks whenever `debug_assertions` is on). Release disassembly now mirrors the C instruction-for-instruction.\n\n## Phases B–D — 26 tests, all green in 4 matrix cells\n\nEverything goes through `libloading` on both `.so`s; the Rust code is never called directly.\n\n| Rust profile | C build | Phase B | Phase C |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| debug | default (`-O0`) | 19 ✓ | 7 ✓ |\n| debug | `-O2` | 19 ✓ | 7 ✓ |\n| release | default | 19 ✓ | 7 ✓ |\n| release | `-O2` | 19 ✓ | 7 ✓ |\n\n~1.2M calls per implementation per cell, plus a 10.24M-call soak test. Results compared as `f64::to_bits` (never `==`) plus full 16-byte post-call state and guard-byte canaries. The `-O2` C build was added because `*(double *)&result` is a type-pun that an optimizer could legally treat differently.\n\nTwo guards against false passes: a **staleness assertion** (`cargo test` does *not* rebuild a `cdylib`-only lib target — this initially hid a fix and would have silently tested an old `.so`), and **mutation testing** (`>> 17` → `>> 18`, and dropping the `state[0]` write; each failed 17 tests).\n\nFiles added: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `verify.sh`, `tests/common/mod.rs`, `tests/phase_b_valid_paths.rs`, `tests/phase_c_error_paths.rs`; `libloading` added to dev-dependencies. `c_src/` sources are byte-identical (only the instructed `build/` dir was created)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":678,"cache_read_input_tokens":97931,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":678,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"841c8d4b-39bd-463c-9376-616c5d19a057","uuid":"74791b03-07f6-47fa-85d8-a6010e14e523","timestamp":"2026-08-19T14:09:21.325Z"}
